      <!-- ============ ZooKeeper (Removed) ============ -->
      <section id="zkeeper" class="py-6 sm:py-10">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-indigo-700 mb-5 text-center">⚠️ ZooKeeper: Removed, Not Deprecated</h2>

        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 text-center max-w-3xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          <strong>Apache ZooKeeper is no longer part of Kafka.</strong> Kafka 4.0 (released March 2025) <strong>eliminated</strong> ZooKeeper entirely — no fallback, no compatibility mode, and no security support.
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 sm:gap-6">
          <div class="p-5 bg-red-50 rounded-2xl shadow-sm">
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-red-800 mb-2">What Changed</h3>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base space-y-1">
              <li><strong>Kafka 3.9</strong> was the last release to support ZooKeeper.</li>
              <li><strong>Kafka 4.0+</strong> is KRaft-only. ZooKeeper mode is gone.</li>
              <li>ZooKeeper security support ended <strong>November 2025</strong>.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="p-5 bg-amber-50 rounded-2xl shadow-sm">
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-amber-800 mb-2">Migration Path (Mandatory)</h3>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base space-y-1">
              <li>Upgrade to <strong>Kafka 3.9</strong> (bridge release).</li>
              <li>Migrate to KRaft <strong>while still on 3.x</strong>.</li>
              <li>Then upgrade to <strong>Kafka 4.x</strong>.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-6 p-5 bg-gray-100 rounded-2xl shadow-sm text-center">
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
            <strong>Why it matters:</strong> ZooKeeper required a separate distributed system to operate, monitor, and secure. Removing it simplifies the stack and unlocks features that were difficult or impossible with ZooKeeper — such as <strong>2 million partitions per cluster</strong> and dramatically faster controller failovers.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ KRaft & Migration ============ -->
      <section id="kraft" class="py-6 sm:py-10">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-indigo-700 mb-6 text-center">⚙️ KRaft: The Only Metadata Mode</h2>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 sm:gap-6">
          <article class="bg-white shadow-sm rounded-2xl p-5">
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-green-700 mb-3">🔹 Controller Quorum</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
              A small set of Kafka nodes (typically <strong>3 or 5</strong>) run the <strong>Raft consensus protocol</strong> to manage all cluster metadata. Dedicated controllers are recommended for production.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="bg-white shadow-sm rounded-2xl p-5">
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-purple-700 mb-3">🔹 Brokers and Controllers</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
              Nodes can be <strong>brokers</strong>, <strong>controllers</strong>, or both. <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">process.roles=broker,controller</code> works for small clusters; <strong>separate controller nodes</strong> isolate metadata load and improve stability.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="bg-white shadow-sm rounded-2xl p-5">
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-blue-700 mb-3">🔹 Scale Benefits</h3>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base space-y-1">
              <li><strong>2 million partitions</strong> (vs ~200k with ZooKeeper).</li>
              <li><strong>Dramatically faster</strong> controller failover.</li>
              <li>Single system to configure and monitor.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>

          <article class="bg-white shadow-sm rounded-2xl p-5">
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-red-700 mb-3">🔹 Pre-Vote (KIP-996)</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
              Pre-vote reduces unnecessary leader elections caused by transient network issues, improving metadata stability.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-5 p-5 sm:p-6 bg-amber-50 rounded-2xl shadow-sm">
          <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-amber-800 mb-3">⚠️ Migration Complexity: Not a Routine Bump</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base mb-2 leading-relaxed">
            Migrating to Kafka 4.0 is a <strong>full architectural overhaul</strong>, not just a version number change. Organizations that start the migration expecting a simple upgrade discover these realities mid-project:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base space-y-1">
            <li><strong>Controller quorum design</strong> must be planned — dedicated controllers, rack awareness, and quorum sizing.</li>
            <li><strong>Tooling and configs</strong> must be rewritten: <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">zookeeper.connect</code>, <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">zookeeper.*</code>, and legacy scripts are gone.</li>
            <li><strong>Deployment automation</strong> (Ansible, Terraform, Helm, Strimzi) needs updated templates.</li>
            <li><strong>Monitoring</strong> must be re-plumbed to KRaft metrics (controller quorum state, metadata lag).</li>
            <li><strong>Rollback is limited</strong> — once the bridge migration completes, going back is non-trivial.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ Tiered Storage ============ -->
      <section id="tiered" class="py-6 sm:py-10">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-5">💾 Tiered Storage (KIP-405, GA since Kafka 3.9)</h2>

        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-gray-700 mb-5 leading-relaxed">
          Tiered storage decouples <strong>compute</strong> from <strong>storage</strong>. Recent data stays on broker disks (<strong>hot tier</strong>), while older segments are offloaded to object storage (<strong>cold tier</strong>). Clients read historical data with <strong>no code changes</strong>.
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 sm:gap-6">
          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm p-5">
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-green-700 mb-2">💰 Cost Savings</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
              Object storage costs a fraction of SSD/NVMe. Organizations report <strong>3–9× storage cost reductions</strong>.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm p-5">
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-blue-700 mb-2">⚡ Operational Gains</h3>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base space-y-1">
              <li><strong>15× faster</strong> partition reassignments.</li>
              <li><strong>13–115× faster</strong> disaster recovery.</li>
              <li><strong>Independent scaling</strong> of compute and storage.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-5 bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm p-5 sm:p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-red-700 mb-3">⚠️ Limitations You Must Know</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Historical read bottleneck (pre-4.2):</strong> Only one partition per fetch could pull from remote storage, capping throughput at ~1 MiB by default. Fixed in Kafka 4.2 with multi-partition remote fetch.</li>
            <li><strong>Minimum retention constraints:</strong> Low-cost object storage tiers often require a <strong>minimum 3-day retention period</strong>. Verify policy before setting <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">local.retention.ms</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Storage backend support:</strong> Only <strong>S3-API-compatible</strong> stores are certified. Non-certified object stores (some on-prem or exotic cloud options) may not be supported.</li>
            <li><strong>Operational visibility:</strong> Remote read latency and error metrics must be actively monitored — they are separate from broker-local metrics.</li>
            <li><strong>Migration of existing topics:</strong> Enabling tiered storage on existing topics does not retroactively offload data — only new segments are tiered.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-5 bg-gray-900 text-green-300 p-4 sm:p-5 rounded-xl shadow-sm overflow-x-auto text-xs sm:text-sm">
<pre class="prose-pre"># Broker-level enablement
remote.log.storage.system.enable=true
remote.log.metadata.manager.class.name=org.apache.kafka.server.log.remote.metadata.storage.TopicBasedRemoteLogMetadataManager

# Topic-level (example)
kafka-topics.sh --create --topic transactions \
  --config remote.storage.enable=true \
  --config local.retention.ms=86400000 \
  --config retention.ms=2592000000</pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ Share Groups ============ -->
      <section id="sharegroups" class="py-6 sm:py-10">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-5">📬 Share Groups (KIP-932): Queues for Kafka</h2>

        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-gray-700 mb-5 leading-relaxed">
          Share groups bring <strong>queue-like semantics</strong> to Kafka. Unlike traditional consumer groups where each partition is assigned to exactly one consumer, <strong>multiple consumers in a share group can read from the same partition cooperatively</strong>, with <strong>per-message acknowledgment</strong>.
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 sm:gap-6">
          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm p-5">
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-indigo-700 mb-2">✅ When to Use</h3>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base space-y-1">
              <li>Point-to-point messaging patterns.</li>
              <li>Workloads previously on RabbitMQ or SQS.</li>
              <li>Elastic consumer scaling beyond partition count.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm p-5">
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-amber-700 mb-2">⚠️ Current Status</h3>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base space-y-1">
              <li><strong>Preview</strong> in Kafka 4.1; <strong>GA in 4.2</strong>.</li>
              <li>Early-access clusters <strong>cannot upgrade to 4.1</strong> — keep out of production until GA.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-5 p-5 bg-red-50 rounded-2xl shadow-sm">
          <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-red-800 mb-3">🚨 Critical Trade-offs</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base space-y-2">
            <li><strong>No per-partition ordering:</strong> Because records are dispatched independently to consumers, share groups do <strong>not</strong> guarantee message order within a partition. Do not use for ordering-sensitive workloads.</li>
            <li><strong>Dead-letter queue (DLQ) gap:</strong> As of late 2026, KIP-1191 (DLQ for share groups) is still in development. Records that exhaust retries are archived but <strong>not delivered to a DLQ</strong>. You must design compensating logic manually.</li>
            <li><strong>New consumer API:</strong> Share groups use a distinct client API (<code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">KafkaShareConsumer</code>) — existing consumer code cannot be reused directly.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ KIP-848 ============ -->
      <section id="kip848" class="py-6 sm:py-10">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-5">⚡ KIP-848: Next-Gen Consumer Rebalance</h2>

        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-gray-700 mb-5 leading-relaxed">
          The classic rebalance protocol causes <strong>“stop-the-world” pauses</strong>. KIP-848 introduces a <strong>server-side, incremental</strong> protocol that delivers <strong>up to 20× faster rebalances</strong> and eliminates full-group synchronization barriers.
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 sm:gap-6">
          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm p-5">
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-green-700 mb-2">How to Enable</h3>
            <pre class="bg-gray-900 text-green-300 p-3 rounded-lg text-xs sm:text-sm overflow-x-auto"><code>group.protocol=consumer</code></pre>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base mt-2">Server-side enabled by default in Kafka 4.0; clients opt in.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm p-5">
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-blue-700 mb-2">Real-World Impact</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base">
              In one benchmark, rebalance time dropped from <strong>103 seconds to 5 seconds</strong>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-5 p-5 bg-amber-50 rounded-2xl shadow-sm">
          <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-amber-800 mb-3">⚠️ Adoption Friction</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Classic protocol is still the default</strong> in <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">KafkaConsumer</code>. Adoption is opt-in, so many production clusters are still running the old protocol.</li>
            <li><strong>No regex subscription support</strong> in the current KIP-848 implementation. If you rely on topic regex subscriptions, KIP-848 will not work for those consumers.</li>
            <li><strong>Alternative Kafka implementations</strong> (e.g., Redpanda) may not support KIP-848 yet. Verify compatibility before enabling in multi-implementation environments.</li>
            <li><strong>Kafka Streams</strong> gained KIP-1071 support in 4.1 — earlier versions cannot use the new protocol with Streams.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ ISR & ELR ============ -->
      <section id="isr" class="py-6 sm:py-10">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-5">🛡️ ISR, ELR and Modern Durability</h2>

        <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm p-5 sm:p-6 mb-5">
          <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-3">Core Settings (Still Valid)</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Replication factor 3</strong> at topic creation.</li>
            <li><strong>min.insync.replicas=2</strong> — not 3 — leaving headroom for maintenance.</li>
            <li><strong>acks=all</strong> on producers for durability.</li>
            <li><strong>enable.idempotence=true</strong> to prevent duplicates during retries.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm p-5 sm:p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-green-700 mb-3">🔹 Eligible Leader Replicas (ELR, KIP-966)</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base mb-3 leading-relaxed">
            ELR is a <strong>subset of ISR</strong> guaranteed to have complete data up to the high watermark. Only ELR replicas are eligible for leader election, <strong>preventing silent data loss</strong> even when ISR is non-empty.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base mb-2"><strong>Why ELR matters — a concrete failure:</strong></p>
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base space-y-1">
            <li>Leader L receives messages and acknowledges to producers before followers fully replicate.</li>
            <li>L crashes; a follower with ISR membership but incomplete data becomes leader.</li>
            <li>Un-replicated messages are permanently lost — producers think they were written.</li>
            <li>ELR restricts leader eligibility to replicas that <strong>provably</strong> have all acknowledged data, closing this gap.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ Throughput & Sizing ============ -->
      <section id="throughput" class="py-6 sm:py-10">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-5">🚀 Producer Throughput & Partition Sizing</h2>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 sm:gap-6">
          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm p-5">
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-green-700 mb-2">✅ Use LZ4 Compression</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
              LZ4 offers the best balance of speed (~594 MB/s compression, ~2,428 MB/s decompression) for high-throughput workloads.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm p-5">
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-red-700 mb-2">⚠️ Avoid ZSTD</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
              ZSTD has <strong>known data-corruption edge cases</strong> in certain librdkafka versions. Stick with LZ4 unless you have validated a specific version.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm p-5">
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-blue-700 mb-2">🔹 Batching</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
              Increase <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">batch.size</code> and <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">linger.ms</code>; compression dramatically reduces network I/O.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm p-5">
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-indigo-700 mb-2">🔹 Idempotence</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
              Always set <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">enable.idempotence=true</code> to preserve ordering and avoid duplicates during retries.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-5 bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm p-5 sm:p-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-3">📐 Partition Count Sizing Framework</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base mb-3 leading-relaxed">
            Rules of thumb (avoid primes, prefer 6/12/24/60, stay under 4,000 partitions per broker) are a starting point, not a sizing method. Use the framework below:
          </p>
          <ol class="list-decimal list-inside text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Target consumer throughput:</strong> Measure single-consumer processing rate (e.g., 5 MB/s). Total desired throughput ÷ consumer rate = minimum partition count.</li>
            <li><strong>Target producer throughput:</strong> Ensure broker count × per-broker write capacity ≥ desired write throughput. Partitions must be spread across brokers to utilize this.</li>
            <li><strong>Replication overhead:</strong> Multiply partition count by replication factor for total replica count. Each broker should hold a balanced share; keep replicas-per-broker under ~4,000.</li>
            <li><strong>Controller and metadata load:</strong> KRaft supports 2M partitions per cluster, but each broker should not exceed ~4,000 partitions unless stress-tested.</li>
            <li><strong>Growth headroom:</strong> Add 30–50% capacity. Partition count cannot be reduced without topic recreation.</li>
            <li><strong>Key cardinality:</strong> If using key-based partitioning, ensure the key space exceeds partition count to avoid hot partitions.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base mt-3 leading-relaxed">
            <strong>Example:</strong> 300 MB/s write traffic, 5 MB/s consumer rate, RF=3. Minimum partitions = 60 for consumer parallelism. Total replicas = 180. Across 9 brokers = ~20 replicas/broker — comfortable. Add 50% headroom → 90 partitions.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ Consumer Lag ============ -->
      <section id="lag" class="py-6 sm:py-10">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-5">📉 Consumer Lag (Still Critical)</h2>

        <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm p-5 sm:p-6">
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Rebalance pauses</strong> are the #1 cause. Migrate to <strong>KIP-848</strong> (<code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">group.protocol=consumer</code>) for up to 20× faster rebalances.</li>
            <li><strong>Partition count bounds parallelism</strong> — adding consumers beyond partition count does not help.</li>
            <li>Monitor lag with <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">kafka-consumer-groups.sh</code> or modern dashboards.</li>
            <li>Blocking operations in the poll loop cause heartbeat failures and rebalances — keep the loop non-blocking.</li>
            <li><strong>Cascading rebalance storms:</strong> When LLM API latency exceeds <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">max.poll.interval.ms</code>, the consumer is evicted, triggering rebalance after rebalance. See AI section below.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ AI / GenAI ============ -->
      <section id="ai" class="py-6 sm:py-10">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-5">🤖 Kafka for AI / GenAI (2025–2026)</h2>

        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-gray-700 mb-5 leading-relaxed">
          Kafka has become the <strong>real-time data backbone</strong> for enterprise AI. It connects operational data with <strong>RAG pipelines</strong>, vector databases, and LLM-powered assistants. Beyond being an "event backbone," there are specific production inference patterns every architect should know.
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm p-5">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-indigo-700 mb-2">1. External RPC</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Consumer calls <strong>OpenAI, Anthropic, or Bedrock</strong> APIs. Simplest pattern. Risk: API latency and rate limits can exceed <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">max.poll.interval.ms</code>.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm p-5">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-green-700 mb-2">2. Embedded Model</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Run <strong>ONNX or TensorFlow Lite</strong> models inside the consumer JVM. Low latency, no network hop. Ideal for classification, filtering, or embedding.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm p-5">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-purple-700 mb-2">3. Sidecar Inference</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              GPU serving (Triton, vLLM, TorchServe) runs <strong>next to the Kafka processor</strong> in the same pod/host. Balances latency and model flexibility.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-5 p-5 bg-red-50 rounded-2xl shadow-sm">
          <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-red-800 mb-3">🚨 Cascading Rebalance Storm (Production Risk)</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base mb-2 leading-relaxed">
            LLM API latency is variable and often exceeds 30 seconds. If a consumer blocks on an LLM call longer than <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">max.poll.interval.ms</code>:
          </p>
          <ol class="list-decimal list-inside text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base space-y-1">
            <li>Broker evicts the consumer → rebalance begins.</li>
            <li>Another consumer picks up the same records → also blocks on the LLM.</li>
            <li>Repeat → the <strong>entire consumer group enters a rebalance storm</strong>.</li>
          </ol>
          <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm sm:text-base mt-3 leading-relaxed">
            <strong>Mitigations:</strong> Use async HTTP clients with bounded timeouts, decouple LLM calls from the poll loop via internal queues, set generous <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1 rounded">max.poll.interval.ms</code> (5–10 min), or use the Kafka Streams async processing model.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-5 grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm p-5">
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-indigo-700 mb-2">Real-Time RAG</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Streaming architecture enables <strong>continuous context updates</strong> for RAG — unlike static knowledge bases that go stale.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-sm p-5">
            <h3 class="text-lg sm:text-xl font-semibold text-purple-700 mb-2">Exactly-Once for AI</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
              Idempotent producers + transactions handle Kafka-side exactly-once. External LLM calls need <strong>idempotency keys</strong> and compensation logic.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

